---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
import { exampleThemeNotes } from "../../utils/example-theme-notes"
import { EXAMPLE_THEMES } from "../../utils/example-themes"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Customizing</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    Where your CSS goes: a theme in <code>theme.custom</code> changes the whole library,
    a component's <code>--_*</code> properties change one component or one instance,
    and your own layer holds the rest.
  </Fragment>

  <section id="your-theme">
    <h2>Your theme</h2>
    <p>
      A theme is the <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#token-tiers"
        >knobs, roles and component tokens</DocLink
      > you change, in the <code>theme.custom</code> layer, right after the defaults.
      Tokens you leave out keep their defaults, also new ones in later releases.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer theme.custom {
  :where(html) {
    --ui-density: 0.9;
    --ui-font-body: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
    --ui-font-heading: "Fraunces", serif;
    --ui-palette-chroma: 0.35;
    --ui-palette-hue: 265;
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      If it might load before <code>layers.css</code>, start it with the two <code
        >@layer</code
      > statements from there, like the example themes do. The <DocLink
        href="/theme-builder">Theme builder</DocLink
      > writes one for you, and <DocLink href="/guide/theming">Theming</DocLink> walks
      through the knobs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Knobs inherit, so <code>html</code> is enough for them. A role you pin, such
      as
      <code>--ui-surface</code> or <code>--ui-primary</code>, goes on <code
        >:where(html, .ui-palette, .ui-contrast-more)</code
      > so the scope classes keep it. The example themes and the Theme builder do
      that for every token.
    </p>
    <h3 id="example-themes">Example themes</h3>
    <p>
      The themes below ship with the package to show how far tokens go. Each is
      only tokens in <code>theme.custom</code>, on <code>html</code> and, for the
      colors of severity scopes such as <code>.ui-info</code>, on those classes.
      No component CSS. Every stress test page runs with each of them in light
      and dark mode. They copy their originals faithfully, also where those fall
      short of WCAG contrast (the translucent focus rings of Bootstrap and
      shadcn, the light field borders of Bootstrap and shadcn, the bright blue
      of brutalist). They name their fonts (Geist, Roboto, DM Sans) but don't
      load them. The <DocLink href="/theme-builder">Theme builder</DocLink> starts
      from any of them.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        EXAMPLE_THEMES.map(({ name }) => {
          const { description, original } = exampleThemeNotes(name)
          return (
            <li>
              <code>{name}</code>: {original}.{" "}
              <Fragment set:html={description} />
            </li>
          )
        })
      }
    </ul>
    <Code
      code={`@import "opui-css/css/imports.css";
@import "opui-css/themes/brutalist.css";`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="component-properties">
    <h2>Component properties</h2>
    <p>
      The <code>--_*</code> private properties are each component's wiring, the last
      <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#token-tiers">tier</DocLink> before its CSS. Every
      component page lists its own under Component properties, marked as inputs or
      outputs, and components share names where they mean the same thing. Button reads
      these:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>--_accent</code>: the accent color. Fills, borders, text, hover
        and active states are all derived from it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_bg-color</code> and <code>--_text-color</code>: the current
        background and text color.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_duration</code> and <code>--_ease</code>: the transition
        duration and easing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_icon-size</code>: the size of a direct child <code>svg</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_size</code>: the control size, set by the size classes: the
        height, and the width when the control is square.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_motion</code>: the motion multiplier, <code>0</code> turns transitions
        off.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_padding-inline</code>: the inline padding, set by the size
        classes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The component declares them on itself, so set them on the component, not
      on a parent. Theme tokens are the ones to set on a parent:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`/* Does nothing: every .ui-button declares its own --_icon-size */
.toolbar {
  --_icon-size: 1.25em;
}

/* Works */
.toolbar .ui-button {
  --_icon-size: 1.25em;
}

/* Theme tokens inherit, so a parent is fine */
.toolbar {
  --ui-button-radius: var(--ui-radius-round);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <Example centered>
      <button
        class="ui-button ui-filled"
        style="--_accent: var(--ui-green)"
        type="button"
      >
        Green filled
      </button>
      <button
        class="ui-button ui-outlined"
        style="--_accent: var(--ui-green)"
        type="button"
      >
        Green outlined
      </button>
    </Example>
    <p>
      Prefer inputs like <code>--_accent</code> over outputs like <code
        >--_bg-color</code
      >. The states set the outputs, so a new <code>--_bg-color</code> freezes the
      background in every state, while a new <code>--_accent</code> flows through
      all of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="unlayered-or-your-own-layer">
    <h2>Unlayered or your own layer</h2>
    <p>
      Unlayered CSS beats every <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#cascade-layers"
        >layer</DocLink
      >, which is fine for most apps. If you use layers yourself, add one after <code
        >utils</code
      >: it beats the library and still loses to your unlayered one-off fixes.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer openprops, theme, normalize, components.prose, components.root, components.extended, components.compat, utils, overrides;
@layer theme.palette, theme.base, theme.custom, theme.severity;

@layer overrides {
  .ui-badge {
    --_size: var(--ui-space-5);
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>
</Guide>
